The book has never touched an exchange because no host it ran on could reach
one. This is that host, generated rather than clicked, so every claim about it
is a line a test can check.

tools/aws_stack.py emits the whole CloudFormation stack (27 resources) and
check() reads the invariants back off it. Each invariant is a way this book
loses money, not an AWS preference:

  DesiredCount 1 + MaximumPercent 100  two tasks are two engines against one
                                       account and one liquidation price; the
                                       AWS default of 200 puts them side by
                                       side on every deploy
  AssignPublicIp DISABLED, no ingress  the health endpoint is unauthenticated
                                       and reports positions and kill-switch
  egress 443 + NFS only                a compromised container reaches nothing
  EFS at /app/state, encrypted, Retain Fargate's disk dies with the task, and
                                       the 0037 ledger with it
  secrets by ARN, wildcard-free IAM    this repo exists downstream of an image
                                       that shipped live keys
  USE_TESTNET is a LITERAL             mainnet is a code change and a review,
                                       never a deploy-time parameter

The checker can fail: seven mutations (two tasks, 200%, a public IP, an open
egress rule, a key in the environment block, an unmounted state directory, a
wildcard resource) each produce a named problem, and the CLI exits 1.

EGRESS, HONESTLY. "NAT allowlist to the venue only" means AWS Network
Firewall with an FQDN rule group: ~$300/month against a $100 book. It is
generated behind --network-firewall and OFF by default. At this size the
controls that protect the money are on the key — withdrawals disabled, pinned
to the NAT's elastic IP, which the stack outputs as NatIpToPinTheKeyTo. The
stack as generated costs about $55/month to run; that is the arithmetic of a
drill, not of a business, and the same template carries a book a thousand
times larger.

The Dockerfile now ships exactly two read-only tools, named one by one
(never `COPY tools/`): connector_check.py and session_tail.py. The first is
the point — `--run-task-command` prints the one-off Fargate task that runs
`connector_check --require-bybit-testnet` from INSIDE the VPC. Exit 0 there
is the first fact this project has never had.

Nothing has been deployed. The template is code, not evidence, and
docs/human/AWS_RUNTIME_0038.md says so in the first three lines, along with
the deploy sequence, the key hygiene, and the six drill steps that follow.

Tests first: bot/tests/test_aws_stack.py, 34 new.

